From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/a733: tcon-tv, kms, dp: round each mode's pixel clock on the CRTC instead of allowing one

sun60i_dp_bridge_mode_valid() refused every mode whose clock was not
exactly 154000 kHz (1920x1200 CVT-RB) with MODE_CLOCK_RANGE. A monitor
without that mode gets an empty mode list and no picture: every EDID mode,
the DP 640x480 fail-safe and the no-EDID 1024x768 fallback fail the same test
(drm_probe_helper.c:651-689), and the shipped command line has no video=.
The comment beside the test named the way out -- a clk_round_rate()
tolerance check owned by TCON_TV1 once a CRTC exists -- and the CRTC exists
since drm-a733-kms-drm-device-for-the-display-pipe.

WHAT CHANGES

  sun60i-tcon-tv.c  TCON_TV1 owns the pixel clock, so it answers for it:
    sun60i_tcon_tv_kms_clock_valid()  clk_round_rate() on "tcon-tv"
      (EDP_TV_CLK): MODE_OK within 0.5 %, MODE_CLOCK_LOW / _HIGH outside it,
      MODE_CLOCK_HIGH above 154000 kHz, MODE_NOCLOCK without a rate. One
      drm.debug KMS line per mode with the asked and the made rate in Hz and
      the error in ppm.
    sun60i_tcon_tv_kms_clock_fixup()  writes the rate the clock tree makes
      into the adjusted mode, clock and crtc_clock, rounded UP to the kHz.
    The enable line prints the asked rate beside the one it got.
  sun60i-kms.c      the CRTC's .mode_valid adds the clock test after the
                    field-width test; a new .mode_fixup calls the fixup.
  sun60i-kms.h      the two prototypes; <drm/drm_modes.h> for the enum.
  sun60i-dp.c       the equality test leaves .mode_valid (interlace and the
                    link budget stay); the MVID check is documented against
                    the adjusted clock; "video stream enabled" carries the
                    kHz the core measures from MVID.

No register write changes at 1920x1200: 154 MHz is exact, the adjusted
clock stays 154000 and the dividers land where they did (N 77, P0 6, M 2).

WHERE THE VALUES COME FROM

Clock tree (A733 UM V1.00, printed pages; the CCU field tables):

  PLL_VIDEO1_CTRL_REG 0x0140, reset 0x4012_6200 (p. 58-59; decimal offset
    320, reset 1074946560): N = PLL_N + 1, PLL_N 0..254 and a minimum
    PLL_N of 11; M1 = PLL_INPUT_DIV + 1, 0..1; P0 = PLL_OUTPUT0_DIV + 1,
    0..7; VIDEO1PLL4X = InputFreq * N / M1 / P0.
  Table 4-2 "PLL Specifications" (p. 20), PLL_VIDEO1 row: Fvco 1.26G..2.52G,
    pre-divider 1/2, post-divider P0 1~8, reference clock 24MHz.
  Table 4-3 (p. 21): VIDEO0~1_PLL, N 99, VCO 2376, 4x 1188.
  EDP_TV_CLK_REG 0x1640 (p. 177-178): EDP_TV_CLK = Clock Source / M / N,
    both FACTOR fields 0..31, CLK_SRC_SEL 001 = VIDEO1PLL4X.

The model in clk-sunxi-ng-a733-video-plls-and-edp-tv-pixel-clock already
matches every one of these (N 12..255, M1 1..2, P0 1..8, VCO 1272..2520 MHz,
EDP N and M 1..32, rate propagation on), so the CCU is not touched.

24, NOT 26 MHz, and silicon agrees: Table 4-2 gives PLL_VIDEO1's reference
as 24 MHz, and with the fields at N 77, M1 1, P0 6, EDP M 2 the DisplayPort
core measures 154.00 MHz from its own MVID -- 0x4902 over NVID 0x8000 at a
270 MHz symbol clock (measured on
the 8 GiB board, 2026-08-19). From 26 MHz those fields would make 166.8 MHz.

THE TOLERANCE, FROM MAINLINE PRACTICE: 0.5 %. sun4i_rgb.c
(SUN4I_RGB_DOTCLOCK_TOLERANCE_PER_MILLE 5: "VESA DMT defines a tolerance of
0.5% on the pixel clock, while the CVT spec reuses that tolerance in its
examples") and sun4i_hdmi_enc.c ("+-0.5% allowed by HDMI spec") ask the same
question the same way, clk_round_rate() against the mode. Rounding the
adjusted clock up to the kHz is rockchip_drm_vop.c's vop_crtc_mode_fixup():
this tree, like Rockchip's, answers with the largest rate not above the
request, so asking again for the rounded-up kHz lands on the same rate.

WHAT THE TREE MAKES, walked through the three mainline routines
(ccu_mp_find_best_with_parent_adj(), clk_divider_bestdiv(), ccu_nm_find_best())
with a port of them, which reproduces the silicon words for 154 MHz exactly:

  mode           asked      made          VCO   N  M1 P0 EDP N/M  PLL_VIDEO1_CTRL EDP_TV_CLK
  1920x1200@60   154000 kHz 154000000 Hz  1848  77 1  6  1/2      0xF9524C00      0x81000001
  1920x1080@60   148500     148500000     2376  99 1  8  1/2      0xF9726200      0x81000001
  1280x1024@60   108000     108000000     1296  54 1  6  1/2      0xF9523500      0x81000001
  1024x768@60     65000      65000000     1560  65 1  8  1/3      0xF9724000      0x81000002
  640x480@60      25175      25173913     2316 193 2  4  1/23     0xF932C002      0x81000016
  1920x1080@59.94 148352    148285714     2076 173 2  7  1/2      0xF962AC02      0x81000001

(bit 24 reads 1 on silicon and is reserved; bit 26, the unused 3X gate, 0.)
Of 32 common CEA/DMT clocks up to 154 MHz, 26 land exactly and six land low
by at most 999 ppm (720x480@60, 27.027 -> 27.000 MHz), all inside 0.5 %.
The same port shows the fixup's round trip lands on the same rate for every
one of them, from three different starting states of the PLL.

THE CEILING: 154000 kHz, the highest pixel clock this pipe has carried, at
one pixel per clock. The UM names no single-pixel limit. Its one pixel-mode
example is 1920x1080@60 at 148.5 MHz in single, 74.25 in dual and 37.125 MHz
in quad pixel mode (15.4.4.1, p. 736) -- an example, not a threshold. A mode
above the ceiling -- 1600x1200@60 at 162 MHz, 2560x1440@60 at 241.5 -- is
refused by name and the connector offers the next mode the sink lists,
instead of a raster at a rate this silicon has never been seen to run.
Raising it is a measurement; above what that finds is PIXEL_MODE,
which sun60i_tcon_tv_kms_check() already names. Only comments in the TCON and
KMS files that said "a mode list wider than one entry" change for it: that
list now exists, and the factor matters above this ceiling.

WHO ELSE SITS ON PLL_VIDEO1. In this tree: nobody. Its one child is
video1-pll4x, whose one child is edp-tv; the DE0 mux lists VIDEO0PLL4X and
VIDEO2PLL4X but not VIDEO1 (ccu-sun60iw2.c), the 3X tap is not modelled, and
the device tree names only CLK_EDP_TV with assigned-clock-parents
CLK_VIDEO1_PLL4X (tcon_tv1). The UM's PLL distribution figure (Figure 4-2,
p. 22) lists what could: EINK, TCONLCD0-1, COMBOPHY0/1, EDP, HDMI, CSI_MASTER,
CSI, ISP, LPC. None has a driver here that selects it; a register readout on the
board reads each one's gate and source field, so "nobody" is measured, not assumed.
LPC is the exception: the figure hangs it on PLL_VIDEO1, but the CCU register
list has no LPC clock register, so there is nothing to read for it.

WHAT ASSUMED 154 MHz (grep of drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i, scripts, the dts):
  - sun60i-dp.c .mode_valid's equality test -- the defect, removed here;
  - sun60i-display.h's constant mode -- the debugfs verbs' default in the TCON,
    the DE and the transmitter; KMS overwrites it with the adjusted mode, so
    it stays for the instruments;
  - worked examples in comments (sun60i-dp.c's TU and MVID arithmetic,
    sun60i-de.c's frame time) and the board test scripts used for the
    by-hand 1920x1200 bring-up; one of them judges PLL_VIDEO1 only
    by enabled + locked;
  - the first stage of a board test script took "PLL_VIDEO1 is not
    0xF9524C00" as "not yet programmed", which a KMS boot in any other mode
    passes falsely; it now judges PLL_EN. Its second stage keeps the 1920x1200
    words, because the debugfs enable verb it drives rasters the built-in mode;
  - no assigned-clock-rates in the dts, no video= on the command line.

WHAT THE VENDOR DOES HERE, AND WHERE WE DIFFER (allwinner-bsp, read):

  vendor                                     ours
  -----------------------------------------  ---------------------------------
  sunxi_edp_connector_mode_valid()           the CRTC asks clk_round_rate()
  (sunxi_drm_edp.c:3056-3110) filters on     for every mode and refuses what
  a blacklist, the DE's maximum clock, an    the dividers cannot make within
  fps cap and lane capability -- and never   0.5 %
  asks the clock tree whether the mode's
  pixel clock can be made
  -----------------------------------------  ---------------------------------
  the TCON sets the rate and then compares:  the rate is settled before the
  DRM_WARN on the HDMI path                  commit, carried in the adjusted
  (sunxi_tcon.c:522-525), DRM_INFO on the    mode, and read back after
  LCD path (:81-85), and carries on either   clk_set_rate(); a miss fails the
  way                                        enable
  -----------------------------------------  ---------------------------------
  MVID read once and printed as the pixel    MVID compared with the rate the
  clock, compared with nothing               dividers were set to
  (trilinear_dp14.c:1638)
  -----------------------------------------  ---------------------------------
  pclk_limit = <200000> in its dtsi and a    one pixel per clock and a 154 MHz
  doubled pixel mode above it                ceiling, named; the vendor number
  (sunxi_drm_edp.c:652-667)                  is a hint for the measurement

Where the vendor is right: it also filters modes on the display engine's
clock (sunxi_drm_edp.c:3084-3089). Here the engine's own enable refuses a
module clock below the mode's pixel clock (sun60i_de_clocks_on()), so that
half is covered, at enable rather than in the mode list.

Verified: applies at fuzz 0 as part of the full series against a freshly
unpacked 6.18.53; compile-check green; drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/ with W=1,
no warning; the new symbols are in the objects. NOT hardware-proven.

What would prove it, as one observable per mode, on a monitor with
1920x1080: modetest sets 1920x1080@60 and a second mode; per mode the
connector list holds it, PLL_VIDEO1_CTRL and EDP_TV_CLK read the words in
the table above, the rate they decode to from 24 MHz equals the TCON's
"pixel clock ... Hz" line, SRC0_MVID computes back to it within 1 % (the
driver's own check) and in practice within one MVID step of 8.24 kHz, and
SRC0_USER_FIFO_STATUS stays zero. Falsified by: a listed mode that is
refused, MVID computing to something else than the decoded rate -- the
clock model contradicting the core's measurement -- or a picture that does
not come.

Assisted-by: Claude
